{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01RWAojHQKCkbBXNg1Pfr1Z2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "Web search results for query: \"TinyStories\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"Computer Science > Computation and Language\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"Link to original article\",\"url\":\"https://www.podbean.[scrubbed:long-base64]\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759\"},{\"title\":\"huggingface.co\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://forum.devtalk.com/t/tinystories-how-small-can-language-models-be-and-still-speak-coherent-english/109566\"},{\"title\":\"Tiny Book of Tiny Stories: Volume 3\",\"url\":\"https://www.hitrecord.org/store/tinystories\"},{\"title\":\"TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?\",\"url\":\"https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories\"},{\"title\":\"lemmy.world\",\"url\":\"https://lemmy.world/post/370834\"},{\"title\":\"tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://lindac.substack.com/t/tiny-stories\"},{\"title\":\"tinystories 1m\",\"url\":\"https://www.promptlayer.com/models/tinystories-1m\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing SimpleStories\",\"url\":\"https://tdooms.github.io/research/simplestories\"},{\"title\":\"r/MachineLearning May 16 '23\",\"url\":\"https://lr.us.psf.lt/r/MachineLearning/comments/13j0spj/r_tiny_language_models_below_10m_parameters_or\"},{\"title\":\"05 tinystories\",\"url\":\"https://desty.github.io/study-tiny-llm/en/part2/05-tinystories/\"},{\"title\":\"meilu.jpshuntong.com\",\"url\":\"https://meilu.jpshuntong.com/url-68747470733a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"a tiny of tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"docs.google.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf\"}]\n\nTinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories, introduced in a 2023 Microsoft Research paper by Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li that asks how small a language model can be and still write coherent English.\n\n- **The dataset:** It was generated by GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 and uses vocabulary that young children would understand. The Hugging Face listing is roneneldan/TinyStories. One source describes it as roughly 2M short stories, though I did not confirm that figure elsewhere.\n- **Why it matters:** The paper reports that models trained on it can produce coherent, multi-paragraph stories with strong grammar. Its largest model, at about 80M parameters, scored near-perfectly on grammar and consistency but fell well behind GPT-4 on creativity.\n- **Evaluation method:** The authors used GPT-4 to grade model output as if a teacher were marking student stories, scoring dimensions such as grammar, creativity, and consistency.\n- **Released models:** Pretrained models are on Hugging Face in sizes from about 1M to 33M parameters, plus TinyStories-Instruct variants.\n- **Follow-ons:** Projects such as SimpleStories build on the idea, and the approach helped popularize synthetic training data more broadly.\n\nThe results also included unrelated material, such as a hitrecord.org book and a writing-game blog that share the name but are not the dataset.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d5d88b15-e468-4547-b114-4e7dc131ae0c", "uuid": "c0153a56-872c-4c8e-9165-0997ba99cb89", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T18:46:18.129Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "TinyStories", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01Xwgseq64C9WCD8oXnX4Tby", "content": [{"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Computer Science > Computation and Language", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Link to original article", "url": "https://www.podbean.[scrubbed:long-base64]"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759"}, {"title": "huggingface.co", "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://forum.devtalk.com/t/tinystories-how-small-can-language-models-be-and-still-speak-coherent-english/109566"}, {"title": "Tiny Book of Tiny Stories: Volume 3", "url": "https://www.hitrecord.org/store/tinystories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories"}, {"title": "lemmy.world", "url": "https://lemmy.world/post/370834"}, {"title": "tiny stories", "url": "https://lindac.substack.com/t/tiny-stories"}, {"title": "tinystories 1m", "url": "https://www.promptlayer.com/models/tinystories-1m"}, {"title": "Introducing SimpleStories", "url": "https://tdooms.github.io/research/simplestories"}, {"title": "r/MachineLearning May 16 '23", "url": "https://lr.us.psf.lt/r/MachineLearning/comments/13j0spj/r_tiny_language_models_below_10m_parameters_or"}, {"title": "05 tinystories", "url": "https://desty.github.io/study-tiny-llm/en/part2/05-tinystories/"}, {"title": "meilu.jpshuntong.com", "url": "https://meilu.jpshuntong.com/url-68747470733a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf"}, {"title": "a tiny of tiny stories", "url": "https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf"}, {"title": "docs.google.com", "url": "https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf"}]}, "TinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories, introduced in a 2023 Microsoft Research paper by Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li that asks how small a language model can be and still write coherent English.\n\n- **The dataset:** It was generated by GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 and uses vocabulary that young children would understand. The Hugging Face listing is roneneldan/TinyStories. One source describes it as roughly 2M short stories, though I did not confirm that figure elsewhere.\n- **Why it matters:** The paper reports that models trained on it can produce coherent, multi-paragraph stories with strong grammar. Its largest model, at about 80M parameters, scored near-perfectly on grammar and consistency but fell well behind GPT-4 on creativity.\n- **Evaluation method:** The authors used GPT-4 to grade model output as if a teacher were marking student stories, scoring dimensions such as grammar, creativity, and consistency.\n- **Released models:** Pretrained models are on Hugging Face in sizes from about 1M to 33M parameters, plus TinyStories-Instruct variants.\n- **Follow-ons:** Projects such as SimpleStories build on the idea, and the approach helped popularize synthetic training data more broadly.\n\nThe results also included unrelated material, such as a hitrecord.org book and a writing-game blog that share the name but are not the dataset."], "durationSeconds": 4.255669611999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01RWAojHQKCkbBXNg1Pfr1Z2", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "rule"}}]}
